BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 308.0 Td (  # The Myth of the Golden Spring) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 298.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 288.0 Td (  In ancient times, two villages stood on opposite sides of a barren valley. Each had little wat) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 278.0 Td (    er, barely enough to survive. ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 268.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 258.0 Td (  One day, a stranger appeared to the first village with a clay vessel. "Pour your water here," ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 248.0 Td (    she said, "and it will flow to your neighbors threefold. But only if you trust them to send ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 238.0 Td (    some portion back.") Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 228.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 218.0 Td (  The villagers were afraid. If they sent their precious water and received nothing in return, t) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 208.0 Td (    hey would perish. But the elders decided: "We must have faith. Abundance cannot grow from cl) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 198.0 Td (    osed hands.") Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 188.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 178.0 Td (  They poured half their water into the vessel. It flowed across the valley and multiplied-three) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 168.0 Td (     times what was sent now filled the second village's wells.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 158.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 148.0 Td (  The second village rejoiced. They faced a choice: keep everything, or honor the gift with retu) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 138.0 Td (    rn? ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 128.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 118.0 Td (  Their wise woman spoke: "Those who sent water to strangers have shown great trust. To betray s) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 108.0 Td (    uch faith would poison the spring itself. We must return generously, so the cycle continues.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 98.0 Td (    ") Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 88.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 78.0 Td (  They sent back a fair portion. When it reached the first village, both communities had more th) Tj ET
