BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 718.0 Td (      # The Myth of the Golden Spring) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 708.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 698.0 Td (      In ancient times, two villages stood on opposite sides of a barren valley. Each had little) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 688.0 Td (         water, barely enough to survive. ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 678.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 668.0 Td (      One day, a stranger appeared to the first village with a clay vessel. "Pour your water her) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 658.0 Td (        e," she said, "and it will flow to your neighbors threefold. But only if you trust them ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 648.0 Td (        to send some portion back.") Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 638.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 628.0 Td (      The villagers were afraid. If they sent their precious water and received nothing in retur) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 618.0 Td (        n, they would perish. But the elders decided: "We must have faith. Abundance cannot grow) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 608.0 Td (         from closed hands.") Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 598.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 588.0 Td (      They poured half their water into the vessel. It flowed across the valley and multiplied-t) Tj ET
